<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Salvation Front (Romania)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1989">1989</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Salvation Front was the body that took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> during the revolution of December 1989, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-iliescu">Ion Iliescu</a> and dominated by former communists, it turned itself into a political party and won the elections of May 1990 by a wide margin, but its use of miners against protesters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> in June 1990 drew criticism. It split in 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-salvation-front-romania.jpg" alt="National Salvation Front (Romania)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Traced by Stannered · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Romania_(1989_revolution).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In March 1989 six prominent members of the Romanian Communist Party wrote an open letter to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a> criticising his abuses of power and his economic policies; it was read on Radio Free Europe. Later in 1989, before the party's 14th Congress, two leaflets signed National Salvation Front circulated, one questioning his management of the economy and his human rights record, the other asking the Congress not to re-elect him. Silviu Brucan said they were written by Alexandru Melian, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> professor with no link to the Front's leaders; Nicolae Militaru claimed that he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-iliescu">Ion Iliescu</a> led a clandestine Front that asked Melian to write the appeal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-iliescu">Ion Iliescu</a> announced the creation of the Front in radio and television addresses on 22 December 1989, after Ceaușescu's overthrow, and the Front proclaimed itself the supreme power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Within four days it formed an interim government with Iliescu as president and Petre Roman as interim prime minister. On 27 December it abolished the one-party system and called for free elections, and on 6 February 1990 it became a political party.</p><p>Anti-Front demonstrations organised by the National Peasants' Party and the National Liberal Party in January and February 1990 degenerated into violence, and Iliescu called on the working class to support the Front against what he called fascist forces; these events became known as the first and second Mineriads. A larger demonstration, the Golaniad, began in April 1990 and lasted 52 days, until the third Mineriad violently dispersed it on 13-15 June. In the elections of 20 May 1990 the Front won 66% of the national vote and Iliescu was elected president with 85%.</p><p>Tension grew between Iliescu and Roman. After the fourth Mineriad in September 1991 Roman was forced to resign on 1 October 1991. On 7 April 1992 Iliescu and many others left the Front and created the Democratic National Salvation Front, later the Social Democratic Party. Roman kept the Front, which became the Democratic Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Social Democratic Party and the Democratic Party, which later became the Democratic Liberal Party and merged into the National Liberal Party in 2014, both descend from the Front. Traian Băsescu entered politics as a Front member and served as Minister of Transportation in several of its governments.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Salvation Front (Romania)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>National Salvation Front</b>, led by Ion Iliescu, took power and won the elections of May 1990, though violent interventions by miners in Bucharest in 1990 cast doubt on its commitment to democracy.</blockquote><a href="/state/romania">Romania</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>National Salvation Front</b> took power, founding post-communist Romania.</blockquote><a href="/state/socialist-republic-of-romania">Socialist Republic of Romania</a></li></ul></section>
